{"causes": ["The emergence and global dispersal of anatomically modern Homo sapiens provided the biological substrate for Upper Paleolithic innovations.", "The development of advanced cognitive abilities, including symbolic thought and complex language, enabled unprecedented cultural and technological creativity.", "Adaptation to diverse and often challenging Ice Age environments spurred the development of specialized tools, hunting techniques, and social organization."], "effects": ["The Upper Paleolithic established the foundational technological and cultural toolkit for all subsequent human societies, including the Mesolithic and Neolithic eras.", "It resulted in the global dispersal and eventual dominance of Homo sapiens across nearly all habitable landmasses, including the Americas and Australia.", "The period fostered the development of complex social structures, sophisticated symbolic expression, and advanced abstract thought, evident in art and ritual."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "homo-sapiens", "sourceName": "Homo Sapiens", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "upper-paleolithic", "targetName": "Upper Paleolithic", "context": "Anatomically modern Homo sapiens were the sole creators and beneficiaries of the Upper Paleolithic's distinctive cultural and technological traditions."}, {"sourceSlug": "upper-paleolithic", "sourceName": "Upper Paleolithic", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "aurignacian-culture", "targetName": "Aurignacian Culture", "context": "The Upper Paleolithic encompasses the Aurignacian culture, characterized by early blade tools and figurative art from approximately 40,000 to 28,000 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "upper-paleolithic", "sourceName": "Upper Paleolithic", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "eurasia", "targetName": "Eurasia", "context": "Many of the most significant Upper Paleolithic cultural developments, including major cave art sites and advanced tool industries, occurred across Eurasia."}, {"sourceSlug": "mesolithic", "sourceName": "Mesolithic", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "upper-paleolithic", "targetName": "Upper Paleolithic", "context": "The Mesolithic period, beginning around 7700 BCE, directly succeeds the Upper Paleolithic, building upon its technological and social foundations."}, {"sourceSlug": "upper-paleolithic", "sourceName": "Upper Paleolithic", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "cave-art-lascaux", "targetName": "Lascaux Cave Art", "context": "The Upper Paleolithic period saw the creation of sophisticated parietal art, exemplified by the Lascaux cave paintings dating to approximately 17,000 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Lascaux, France", "role": "Major Art Site"}, {"name": "Altamira, Spain", "role": "Major Art Site"}, {"name": "Dolní Věstonice, Czech Republic", "role": "Archaeological Excavation"}], "texts": [], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q479505", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upper_Paleolithic"], "tags": ["historical period", "Paleolithic & Mesolithic", "periodization", "political-history"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Upper Paleolithic left a notable mark on Prehistoric history, documented across 54 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Defined subsequent political and social structures.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 9, "significanceNarrative": "The Upper Paleolithic marks a world-changing epoch where *Homo sapiens* achieved global dominance and developed the cognitive and technological foundations for all subsequent human civilization. It directly led to the colonization of new continents and the emergence of complex symbolic thought.", "significanceCategory": "world-changing"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-30T07:33:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28427760142", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Subdivision of the paleolithic or old stone age Part of Paleolithic. (38000 BCE – 7700 BCE)", "newValue": "The Upper Paleolithic, spanning approximately 38,000 BCE to 7,700 BCE, represents a pivotal subdivision of the Old Stone Age, marked by the global expansion and cultural efflorescence of anatomically modern *Homo sapiens*. This era witnessed a profound "cognitive revolution," distinguishing human behavior from earlier hominins through unprecedented innovation in technology, art, and social organization. It is the period during which humans became the dominant hominin species, adapting to diverse environments across continents from the Ice Age tundra to emerging coastlines.

During this epoch, humanity developed sophisticated tools far beyond the Acheulean handaxes of their predecessors, including specialized blade technologies, microliths, bone and antler tools, and projectile points such as those found in the Solutrean and Magdalenian cultures of Europe. Iconic achievements include the creation of breathtaking parietal art in caves like Lascaux (c. 17,000–15,000 BCE) and Altamira (c. 36,000 BCE – 13,000 BCE), as well as portable art like the Venus figurines. The development of tailored clothing, complex hunting strategies for megafauna, and early forms of ritualistic behavior further underscore the advanced cognitive and social capabilities of Upper Paleolithic peoples.

The legacy of the Upper Paleolithic is immense, forming the bedrock for all subsequent human technological, social, and cultural development. It saw the colonization of Australia and the Americas, setting the stage for vast linguistic and genetic diversity. The innovations in tool-making and resource exploitation provided the adaptive flexibility necessary for humanity to thrive in changing climatic conditions, directly influencing the transition into the Mesolithic and eventually the Neolithic Revolutions. The symbolic expression found in art and personal adornment laid the groundwork for complex communication and abstract thought."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-30T07:33:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28427760142", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Convergence of political, economic, and cultural forces"], "newValue": ["The emergence and global dispersal of anatomically modern Homo sapiens provided the biological substrate for Upper Paleolithic innovations.", "The development of advanced cognitive abilities, including symbolic thought and complex language, enabled unprecedented cultural and technological creativity.", "Adaptation to diverse and often challenging Ice Age environments spurred the development of specialized tools, hunting techniques, and social organization."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-30T07:33:53Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28427760142", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Defined subsequent political and social structures"], "newValue": ["The Upper Paleolithic established the foundational technological and cultural toolkit for all subsequent human societies, including the Mesolithic and Neolithic eras.", "It resulted in the global dispersal and eventual dominance of Homo sapiens across nearly all habitable landmasses, including the Americas and Australia.", "The period fostered the development of complex social structures, sophisticated symbolic expression, and advanced abstract thought, evident in art and 